    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="GPT-3.5-Turbo" id="0">
      <data key="d3">investigate</data>
      <data key="d4">We empirically investigate this question via controlled experiments with a variety of state-of- the-art open (MPT-30B-Instruct, LongChat-13B (16K)) and closed (OpenAI’s GPT-3.5-Turbo and Anthropic’s Claude-1.3) language models in set- tings that require accessing and using information within an input context</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="GPT-3.5-Turbo" id="1">
      <data key="d3">claude-1.3</data>
      <data key="d4">Model Closed-Book Oracle LongChat-13B (16K) 35.0% 83.4% MPT-30B-Instruct 31.5% 81.9% GPT-3.5-Turbo 56.1% 88.3% GPT-3.5-Turbo (16K) 56.0% 88.6% Claude-1.3 48.3% 76.1% Claude-1.3 (100K) 48.2% 76.4% Table 1: Closed-book and oracle accuracy of language models on the multi-document question answering task</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="LongChat-13B" id="0">
      <data key="d3">investigate</data>
      <data key="d4">We empirically investigate this question via controlled experiments with a variety of state-of- the-art open (MPT-30B-Instruct, LongChat-13B (16K)) and closed (OpenAI’s GPT-3.5-Turbo and Anthropic’s Claude-1.3) language models in set- tings that require accessing and using information within an input context</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="LongChat-13B" id="1">
      <data key="d3">claude-1.3</data>
      <data key="d4">Model Closed-Book Oracle LongChat-13B (16K) 35.0% 83.4% MPT-30B-Instruct 31.5% 81.9% GPT-3.5-Turbo 56.1% 88.3% GPT-3.5-Turbo (16K) 56.0% 88.6% Claude-1.3 48.3% 76.1% Claude-1.3 (100K) 48.2% 76.4% Table 1: Closed-book and oracle accuracy of language models on the multi-document question answering task</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="MPT-30B" id="0">
      <data key="d3">compare</data>
      <data key="d4">Figure 10 compares the multi-document QA performance of MPT-30B and MPT-30B-Instruct as a function of the position of the relevant in-</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="MPT-30B" id="1">
      <data key="d3">instruct</data>
      <data key="d4">1st 5th 10th 15th 20th Position of Document with the Answer 44 46 48 50 52 54 56 Accuracy 20 Total Retrieved Documents (~4K tokens) mpt-30b mpt-30b-instruct Figure 10: Multi-document QA performance of MPT- 30B-Instruct compared against its base model (i.e., be- fore instruction fine-tuning) MPT-30B</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="MPT-30B" id="2">
      <data key="d3">see</data>
      <data key="d4">Surprisingly, we see that both MPT-30B and MPT-30B-Instruct ex- hibit a U-shaped performance curve, where perfor- mance is highest when relevant information occurs at the very beginning or very end of the context</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="MPT-30B-Instruct" target="positional bias" id="0">
      <data key="d3">see</data>
      <data key="d4">In addi- tion, we see that the Llama-2 supervised fine-tuning and reinforcement learning from human feedback procedure slightly mitigates the positional bias in smaller models (13B, akin to trends shown when comparing MPT-30B and MPT-30B-Instruct), but minimally affects trends on larger models (70B)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GPT-3.5-Turbo" target="LongChat-13B" id="0">
      <data key="d3">investigate</data>
      <data key="d4">We empirically investigate this question via controlled experiments with a variety of state-of- the-art open (MPT-30B-Instruct, LongChat-13B (16K)) and closed (OpenAI’s GPT-3.5-Turbo and Anthropic’s Claude-1.3) language models in set- tings that require accessing and using information within an input context</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="GPT-3.5-Turbo" target="LongChat-13B" id="1">
      <data key="d3">claude-1.3</data>
      <data key="d4">Model Closed-Book Oracle LongChat-13B (16K) 35.0% 83.4% MPT-30B-Instruct 31.5% 81.9% GPT-3.5-Turbo 56.1% 88.3% GPT-3.5-Turbo (16K) 56.0% 88.6% Claude-1.3 48.3% 76.1% Claude-1.3 (100K) 48.2% 76.4% Table 1: Closed-book and oracle accuracy of language models on the multi-document question answering task</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wikipedia" target="NaturalQuestions-Open" id="0">
      <data key="d3">find</data>
      <data key="d4">When re- trieving from Wikipedia to answer queries from NaturalQuestions-Open, we find that model perfor- mance saturates long before retriever recall satu- rates, indicating that current models fail to effec- tively use additional retrieved documents—using 50 documents instead of 20 retrieved documents only marginally improves performance (∼1.5% for GPT-3.5-Turbo and ∼1% for claude-1.3)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wikipedia" target="GPT-3.5-Turbo" id="0">
      <data key="d3">find</data>
      <data key="d4">When re- trieving from Wikipedia to answer queries from NaturalQuestions-Open, we find that model perfor- mance saturates long before retriever recall satu- rates, indicating that current models fail to effec- tively use additional retrieved documents—using 50 documents instead of 20 retrieved documents only marginally improves performance (∼1.5% for GPT-3.5-Turbo and ∼1% for claude-1.3)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wikipedia" target="multi-document question answering" id="0">
      <data key="d3">do</data>
      <data key="d4">2.1 Experimental Setup In the multi-document question answering task, the model inputs are (i) a question to answer and (ii) k documents (e.g., passages from Wikipedia), where exactly one of the documents contains the answer 1nelsonliu.me/papers/lost-in-the-middle to the question and k −1 “distractor” documents do not</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Wikipedia" target="NaturalQuestions" id="0">
      <data key="d3">use</data>
      <data key="d4">To obtain a document that answers the question, we use the Wikipedia paragraph that contains the answer from the NaturalQuestions annotations</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NaturalQuestions-Open" target="GPT-3.5-Turbo" id="0">
      <data key="d3">find</data>
      <data key="d4">When re- trieving from Wikipedia to answer queries from NaturalQuestions-Open, we find that model perfor- mance saturates long before retriever recall satu- rates, indicating that current models fail to effec- tively use additional retrieved documents—using 50 documents instead of 20 retrieved documents only marginally improves performance (∼1.5% for GPT-3.5-Turbo and ∼1% for claude-1.3)</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="NaturalQuestions-Open" target="Wikipedia" id="0">
      <data key="d3">take</data>
      <data key="d4">A retrieval system (Contriever, fine- tuned on MS-MARCO) takes an input query from NaturalQuestions-Open and returns the k docu- ments from Wikipedia with the highest relevance score</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="Multi-Document Question Answering" target="multi-document question answering" id="0">
      <data key="d3">be</data>
      <data key="d4">To facilitate further work on understanding and improving how language models use their input context, we release our code and evaluation data.1 2 Multi-Document Question Answering Our goal is to better understand how language mod- els use their input context</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="multi-document question answering" target="MPT-30B-Instruct" id="0">
      <data key="d3">claude-1.3</data>
      <data key="d4">Model Closed-Book Oracle LongChat-13B (16K) 35.0% 83.4% MPT-30B-Instruct 31.5% 81.9% GPT-3.5-Turbo 56.1% 88.3% GPT-3.5-Turbo (16K) 56.0% 88.6% Claude-1.3 48.3% 76.1% Claude-1.3 (100K) 48.2% 76.4% Table 1: Closed-book and oracle accuracy of language models on the multi-document question answering task</data>
    </edge>
